662714.description:
Charlotte marks the 70th white shark tagged in our North Atlantic White Shark Study. She is named after the wonderful people of North Carolina that have been so hospitable to us during our Carolinas-based expeditions.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&quot;Charlotte is a young teenager, not yet entering sexual maturity. With her 10 year acoustic tag, 5 year SPOT tag and 1 year PSAT tag we will be able to track Charlotte as she grows into full adulthood,&quot; stated Dr. Bob Hueter, OCEARCH Chief Scientist.


&nbsp;

1480119.description:
Our longtime partner Costa Sunglasses was founded in Ormond Beach, Florida, by a group of anglers committed to protecting our watery world. To celebrate their 40th Anniversary and to applaud our friends on their ongoing conservation efforts, we&rsquo;ve named our first shark tagged on Expedition Southbound Ormond.&nbsp;


We studied Ormond boat side from the Contender due to an issue on our shark lift. We were still able to collect all the critical samples needed by our science team.&nbsp;


Ormond is our 85th white shark sampled, tagged and released in the Western North Atlantic and will provide data for 24 different science projects.

2431784.description:
Say hello to Hanna, a newly tagged tiger shark! She measured 11-feet, 11-inches in length with an estimated weight of 722 pounds. Hanna was sampled approximately 30 nautical miles off the coast of Jacksonville, Florida. During the tagging process, an ultrasound confirmed that Hanna is pregnant, offering OCEARCH a rare opportunity to learn more about tiger shark reproduction.


She was named after Hanna Park in Mayport, Florida &mdash; a special location that also marks the future home of the OCEARCH Global Headquarters. Strong, powerful, and full of life, Hanna&rsquo;s name ties our work in the ocean to our community on shore. Follow Hanna&rsquo;s journey in the OCEARCH Global Shark Tracker and see where she takes us next!


&nbsp;

2794989.description:
Meet Quint, a newly tagged white shark marking a major milestone in OCEARCH history.


On July 23rd, 2025, sub-adult male Quint was tagged and released in Mahone Bay, Nova Scotia. He measured 9 feet 8 inches in length and weighed an estimated 587 lbs at the time of tagging by the Tancook Islands Marine Field Station Team, in collaboration with the OCEARCH Science and Fishing Teams.&nbsp;


Quint is more than just another data point&mdash;he&rsquo;s a symbol of legacy. Named in honor of Dr. Quinton White, founding Executive Director of the Marine Science Research Institute (MSRI) at Jacksonville University, Quint pays tribute to a longtime friend and partner of OCEARCH. Dr. White has been instrumental in pioneering the collaboration between MSRI and OCEARCH, helping bridge science, education, and real-world marine research.


Even more special, Quint is the 100th white shark OCEARCH has tagged, sampled, and released in the western North Atlantic white shark population. His tagging marks a major achievement in our ongoing mission to understand and protect this vital apex predator.


&ldquo;When we started, not one white shark had been tagged in the Western North Atlantic by OCEARCH&mdash;and tagging 100 felt impossible. But inch by inch, with grit, teamwork, and belief, we got there.


Quint marks #100&mdash;a milestone powered by our crew, supported by global scientists, and shared openly with the world.


We&rsquo;re doing this so our great-grandchildren inherit an ocean full of life.&rdquo;


&mdash; Chris Fischer, OCEARCH Founder &amp; Expedition Leader


As Quint begins his journey through the North Atlantic, he carries with him the legacy of scientific partnership, educational impact, and a growing body of knowledge that will help shape the future of ocean conservation. Stay tuned as we follow his movements and continue to learn from this extraordinary shark.


&nbsp;


&nbsp;

2812557.description:
This turtle was tagged by the Archie Carr Center for Sea Turtle Research in partnership with the Caretta Research Project. Her name was given by the &ldquo;Adopt a Turtle&rdquo; initiative.


Wassaw Will was first seen and tagged in 2010 by CRP researchers, and visited us again in 2013, 2016, 2019, 2022, 2025. She typically lays 5 or 6 nests a year, resulting in 31 total nests with 2845 eggs, 2184 of which have hatched! &nbsp;At least 3 documented nests have been lost to storms or tidal surge.&nbsp; Through the genetics project run by Dr. Brain Shamblin at UGA, we have determined that Wassaw Will does not have any known relatives in the North Atlantic Recovery Unit at this time.


&nbsp;
